You are tasked with implementing a simple benchmarking tool in Java using the JMH (Java Microbenchmarking Harness) library. The JMH library provides a framework for creating, running, and analyzing microbenchmarks written in Java. Your goal is to create a benchmark to compare the performance of two different sorting algorithms: Bubble Sort and Quick Sort.

Your benchmarking tool should measure the time taken by each sorting algorithm to sort an array of integers. The benchmark should be designed to run multiple iterations and produce reliable results. The comparison should help determine which sorting algorithm performs better for a given input size.

Your task is to create a Java class that uses JMH annotations to define the benchmarking methods and configure the benchmarking parameters. You should also implement the sorting algorithms (Bubble Sort and Quick Sort) as separate methods within the benchmarking class.

Your implementation should include the following:
- A class annotated with `@State(Scope.Benchmark)` to indicate that the benchmark state should be shared across all threads.
- Methods annotated with `@Benchmark` to measure the performance of the sorting algorithms.
- Configuration for the benchmark iterations, input size, and other relevant parameters.
- Implementation of Bubble Sort and Quick Sort algorithms to sort an array of integers.
- Proper setup and teardown methods to prepare the benchmark state.

Write the benchmarking class and sorting algorithms, ensuring that the benchmark produces accurate and reliable results for comparing the performance of Bubble Sort and Quick Sort.

### Solution:
```java
import org.openjdk.jmh.annotations.*;
import org.openjdk.jmh.runner.Runner;
import org.openjdk.jmh.runner.RunnerException;
import org.openjdk.jmh.runner.options.Options;
import org.openjdk.jmh.runner.options.OptionsBuilder;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

@State(Scope.Benchmark)
public class SortingBenchmark {

    @Param({"1000", "10000", "100000"})
    private int size;

    private int[] data;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws RunnerException {
        Options opt = new OptionsBuilder()
                .include(SortingBenchmark.class.getSimpleName())
                .forks(1)
                .warmupIterations(3)
                .measurementIterations(5)
                .build();

        new Runner(opt).run();
    }

    @Setup
    public void setup() {
        data = ThreadLocalRandom.current().ints(size).toArray();
    }

    @Benchmark
    public void bubbleSortBenchmark() {
        int[] arr = Arrays.copyOf(data, data.length);
        bubbleSort(arr);
    }

    @Benchmark
    public void quickSortBenchmark() {
        int[] arr = Arrays.copyOf(data, data.length);
        quickSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private void quickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pi = partition(arr, low, high);
            quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
            quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
        }
    }

    private int partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = arr[high];
        int i = (low - 1);
        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < pivot) {
                i++;
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }
        int temp = arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] = arr[high];
        arr[high] = temp;
        return i + 1;
    }
}
```

In this solution, we create a Java class `SortingBenchmark` that uses JMH annotations to define benchmarking methods and configure benchmarking parameters. The `@State(Scope.Benchmark)` annotation indicates that the benchmark state should be shared across all threads.

The `@Param` annotation is used to specify the input size for the benchmark. The `@Setup` method initializes the array of random integers for each benchmark iteration.

The `@Benchmark` annotated methods `bubbleSortBenchmark` and `quickSortBenchmark` measure the performance of Bubble Sort and Quick Sort algorithms, respectively. The `bubbleSort` and `quickSort` methods implement the sorting algorithms.

The `main` method configures the benchmark options and runs the benchmark using the JMH Runner.

This benchmarking tool provides a reliable comparison of the performance of Bubble Sort and Quick Sort for different input sizes, helping to determine which sorting algorithm performs better in terms of execution time.

